==========================================
DAY 20 NOTES — File Transfer: scp & rsync
Date: 18-09-2026
==========================================

WHAT I LEARNED TODAY:
- scp re-copies EVERYTHING every time; rsync only transfers what actually CHANGED
- rsync -a (archive) preserves permissions, timestamps, symlinks — use it basically always
- rsync -avz is the standard combo: archive + verbose + compress
- --dry-run PREVIEWS a sync without actually doing it — critical before using --delete
- --delete makes destination an EXACT mirror of source (removes extra files) — dangerous without dry-run first
- Trailing slash on SOURCE matters: folder/ copies contents, folder (no slash) copies the folder itself
- --exclude skips specific files/patterns during a sync

COMMANDS I PRACTICED:
[ ] scp (recap)
[ ] rsync -av (local sync)
[ ] rsync -avz (remote sync over SSH)
[ ] rsync --dry-run
[ ] rsync --exclude

fill this in after running commands yourself :
$ rsync -av practice/ practice_backup/
> 

$ rsync -av practice/ practice_backup/ (run again, after no changes)
> 

$ rsync -avz --dry-run practice/ raven@<server>:/home/raven/backup/
> 


